{
  "dom_id": "step-q130077",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})={e}^{x}+1$ and $L({y}_{2})=2{e}^{x}-3$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2})?$",
  "worked_solution": "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}-{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})-L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2({e}^{x}+1)-(2{e}^{x}-3) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+2-2{e}^{x}+3 \\\\  & =(2{e}^{x}-2{e}^{x})+(2+3) \\\\  & =0+5 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
